{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>For every matter of trespass</b> — But this is not the verse given to me. I was asked about verse 8, not verse 6. Let me re-read the instruction. The user wrote \"Ramban on Exodus 22:8\" and the Hebrew lemma begins with <b>אֲשֶׁר יֹאמַר כִּי הוּא זֶה</b>, which is indeed from Exodus 22:8. The heading and content concern that verse. Let me translate properly:\n\n<b>Which he shall say, 'This is it.'</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 22:8), according to its plain sense: whom the witness shall say, 'This is it' — that which you swore upon, and here it is in your possession. And our Sages said (Bava Kamma 106a): 'This is it' — to teach that they do not obligate him with an oath unless he admitted partially, 'Thus and thus I owe, and the remainder was stolen from me.' And this matter that the Rav wrote is the view of individual sages and is not the halakhah (Bava Kamma 106a–107a), for bailees do not require partial denial and partial admission; even when he claims the claim of theft regarding the entire deposit, he is obligated to swear the oath of bailees. And it is further clarified in the Gemara with definitive proofs that when he denies the principal of the deposit and says, 'You did not deposit anything with me,' if he denies everything he is exempt, and if he admits partially he is obligated to swear — this is according to everyone, even though the Rav does not say so explicitly in his commentary on the Gemara (Bava Kamma 107a, s.v. ma'iz).\n\nIf so, we may say that this verse, according to the midrash of the Sages, concerns one who claims, 'He never deposited anything with me.' For if he denied everything he is exempt, and if he admitted partially and denied partially he is obligated to swear. And [regarding] 'If the thief be not found, then the master of the house shall come near to Elohim' — for every claim of negligence that he raises against him, 'You were negligent in the safekeeping,' or which the bailee shall say, 'This is the deposit that is in my hand, and you did not deposit more with me' — the deposit about which they convict him, that Elohim shall make him pay double to his fellow. And behold, both of them pay, but the double payment applies only to one who claims the claim of theft. And the claim of partial admission applies to all claims, even to a loan, and robbery, and other matters. And in these laws the verse is brief and the halakhot are numerous; there is no need to explain them here except for the purpose of settling the verses."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:09.123902201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}